@ 17.7.2.1.5 - Early Modern English to American English
# citation: Pogostick Man, from FireSpeakerWiki contributors (2013), “English sound changes”. FireSpeakerWiki. <http://wiki.firespeaker.org/English_sound_changes; and my Phonetic Description class> Stuff regarding syllabification (e.g., of /ɹ/) and hiatus Loss of pretonic /ə/ in #U (ongoing)
	æ > æ:[+long] (e.g., NYC) or ɑ:[+long] (e.g., Boston) / _{F:[-voice],N:[-voice]} (“words which change vary between dialects”)
	ɒ > ɒ:[+long] / _F:[-voice]
	ɒ:[+long] > ɔ:[+long] / _F:[-voice]
	æ:[+long] ɑ:[+long] ɔ:[+long] > æə~eə ɑ ɔ
	ʍ > w (regional)
	l > ɫ / “in some conditions”
	ɪ > i / _# when unstressed
	{t,d} > ɾ / V_V:[-stress]
	i u e > ɪ ʊ ɛ / _ɹ
	o ɔ > ɔ ɒ / _ɹ (most dialects have at least one if not both)
	æ > ɛ / _ɹ
	j > ∅ / {θ,s,z,l,n,t,d}_ when in onset position
	ɒ > ɔ / _[+ cons, - fr, + bk, + hi, - lo] “(partial)”
	æ > ɛə / _{n,m} “and others depending on dialect”
	eə > e:[+long]
	iə > ɪ (ongoing)
	w > ∅ / C_ɹ for some C (toward(s), quart(er), sword)
	t > ∅ / f_n̩